---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import { Card, Grid, Timeline, TimelineItem } from "webcoreui/astro";
import DecklistTooltip from "@components/DecklistTooltip.astro";
import DeckSection from "@components/DeckSection.astro";
import { tomVanDeLogtDeck, achiIIesDeck } from "@data/decklists";
---

<Layout
    title="History - Replenish Guide"
    description="Explore the competitive evolution of the Replenish archetype in Magic: The Gathering. Trace its legacy from Type 2 dominance in 2000 to its modern engineering and championship adaptation in Premodern."
>
    <PageHeader
        title="History of the Deck"
        description="From its dominance in Type 2 (Standard) in 2000 to its continuous adaptation after format bans, Replenish has over twenty years of tactical evolution. Explore the key milestones that shaped the archetype."
        imageSrc="/src/assets/banners/parallax_wave.jpeg"
        imageAlt="Parallax Wave"
        imageCaption="Parallax Wave by Greg Staples"
        isCard={true}
    />
    <div style="margin-top: 3rem; margin-bottom: 3rem;">
        <Timeline>
            <TimelineItem title="1. The Standard Origins (2000)" titleTag="h2">
                <p>
                    Replenish first broke through on Magic's biggest stage
                    during the 2000 World Championship, where Hall of Famer Tom
                    van de Logt piloted the strategy to a Top 8 finish. This
                    classic Type 2 build used artifact acceleration like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Sky Diamond">Sky Diamond</a
                    > alongside active mana denial via <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Rishadan Port">Rishadan Port</a
                    > to stall opposing development. A dedicated tutor package of
                    <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mystical Tutor">Mystical Tutor</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Enlightened Tutor">Enlightened Tutor</a
                    > provided excellent game-to-game consistency, while a single
                    copy of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Energy Field">Energy Field</a
                    > acted as a hard defensive stop against aggro while the deck
                    assembled its combo.
                </p>

                <Card>
                    <div
                        style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
                    >
                        <div>
                            <h3
                                style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                            >
                                2000 World Championship (Standard)
                            </h3>
                            <p
                                style="margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-40);"
                            >
                                Played by Tom van de Logt
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            tomVanDeLogtDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem
                title="2. The Parallax Tide Era (Pre-Ban)"
                titleTag="h2"
            >
                <p>
                    As Premodern grew into a definitive competitive eternal
                    format, deckbuilders quickly realized that <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Parallax Tide">Parallax Tide</a
                    > could function as a one-sided Armageddon. By running a definitive
                    four-of suite of <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Seal of Cleansing">Seal of Cleansing</a
                    >, pilots could activate Tide to target five opposing lands,
                    and then immediately sacrifice Seal to destroy Tide <em
                        >in response to its own exile triggers</em
                    >. Because Tide left the battlefield before its initial
                    abilities resolved, its return-to-play clause would resolve
                    while nothing had been exiled yet. Once the stack cleared,
                    the target lands were removed permanently—no <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Opalescence">Opalescence</a
                    > required.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Backed by maindeck disruption like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Tsabo's Web">Tsabo's Web</a
                    > to paralyze utility mana bases, Parallax Tide Replenish became
                    an undisputed tier-1 powerhouse right up until format curation
                    intervened.
                </p>
                <Card>
                    <div
                        style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;"
                    >
                        <div>
                            <h3
                                style="margin: 0; font-size: 1.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                            >
                                Parallax Tide Lock Architecture
                            </h3>
                            <p
                                style="margin: 0.25rem 0 0 0; color: var(--w-color-primary-40);"
                            >
                                Piloted by AchiIIes (Pre-Ban)
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>

                    <Grid column={{ md: 3 }} gap="lg">
                        {
                            achiIIesDeck.columns.map((col) => (
                                <div>
                                    {col.sections.map((sec) => (
                                        <DeckSection
                                            title={sec.title}
                                            items={sec.items}
                                        />
                                    ))}
                                </div>
                            ))
                        }
                    </Grid>
                </Card>
            </TimelineItem>

            <TimelineItem title="3. The Post-Ban Resurgence" titleTag="h2">
                <p>
                    When format curator Martin Berlin officially banned Parallax
                    Tide to curb repetitive, non-interactive land locks and
                    dominant tournament win rates, many players quickly counted
                    the deck out—assuming Replenish could not survive without
                    its primary mana-denial combo.
                </p>
                <p>
                    However, dedicated pilots adapted quickly. While Replenish has always been at its core a classic combo-control deck, losing Tide simply meant reallocating those slots toward alternative locks and interaction. By shifting the primary endgame piece to <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Decree of Silence">Decree of Silence</a
                    > and reinforcing the maindeck with interactive permission spells like <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Abeyance">Abeyance</a
                    > and <a
                        href="#"
                        class="card-hover"
                        data-card-name="Mana Leak">Mana Leak</a
                    >, the archetype proved it didn't need early land destruction to maintain its position as a tournament heavyweight.
                </p>
            </TimelineItem>
        </Timeline>
    </div>
    <DecklistTooltip />
</Layout>
